    17	| SOCKS5h / proxy DNS | `curl --socks5-hostname` or `socks5h://` | The hostname reaches the SSH server and the remote SSH machine resolves it. Use this for names known only to the remote VPN. |
    18	
    19	Look for a client setting named **proxy DNS**, **remote DNS**, or **SOCKS5h**.
    20	An application that uses its own DoH resolver or ignores SOCKS settings may not
    21	follow this behavior.
    22	
    23	## Run privacy-safe checks
    24	
    25	Use an authorized synthetic service or organization-owned health endpoint. Do
    26	not put private keys, certificates, tokens, cookies, authorization headers,
    27	raw route tables, or full resolver output into a ticket. Use stable redactions
    28	such as `<PRIVATE_V4>`, `<PRIVATE_HOST>`, `<TCP_PORT>`, and `<RELUX_LOCAL_PORT>`.
    29	Record only each command's exit code and the relevant error phrase.
    30	
    31	Set placeholders independently on the source and remote Macs:
    32	
    33	```bash
    34	TARGET_IP='<PRIVATE_V4>'
    35	TARGET_HOST='<PRIVATE_HOST>'
    36	TARGET_PORT='<TCP_PORT>'
    37	SOCKS_PORT='<RELUX_LOCAL_PORT>'
    38	```
    39	
    40	On the remote Mac, signed in as the SSH account, first prove the exact TCP
    41	service can be reached directly:
    42	
    43	```bash
    44	/usr/bin/nc -vz -G 5 "$TARGET_IP" "$TARGET_PORT"
    45	/usr/bin/nc -vz -G 5 "$TARGET_HOST" "$TARGET_PORT"
    46	/sbin/route -n get "$TARGET_IP"
    47	```
    48	
    49	On the source Mac, check the local listener and then use an authorized
    50	plain-HTTP health path for an IP-versus-hostname comparison. Plain HTTP avoids
    51	an HTTPS certificate-name mismatch when testing a raw IP address.
    52	
    53	```bash
    54	/usr/bin/nc -vz -G 5 127.0.0.1 "$SOCKS_PORT"
    55	
    56	/usr/bin/curl --silent --show-error --output /dev/null \
    57	  --connect-timeout 5 --max-time 10 \
    58	  --socks5 "127.0.0.1:$SOCKS_PORT" \
    59	  "http://$TARGET_IP:$TARGET_PORT/<HEALTH_PATH>"
    60	
    61	/usr/bin/curl --silent --show-error --output /dev/null \
    62	  --connect-timeout 5 --max-time 10 \
    63	  --socks5-hostname "127.0.0.1:$SOCKS_PORT" \
    64	  "http://$TARGET_HOST:$TARGET_PORT/<HEALTH_PATH>"
    65	
    66	/usr/bin/curl --silent --show-error --output /dev/null \
    67	  --connect-timeout 5 --max-time 10 \
    68	  --socks5 "127.0.0.1:$SOCKS_PORT" \
    69	  "http://$TARGET_HOST:$TARGET_PORT/<HEALTH_PATH>"
    70	```
    71	
    72	Interpret the three source checks in order:
    73	
    74	1. Numeric IP through SOCKS tests remote TCP routing without destination DNS.
    75	2. `--socks5-hostname` tests remote/proxy DNS for the private hostname.
    76	3. Hostname with `--socks5` is the deliberate source-Mac local-DNS comparison.
    77	
    78	If the proxy-DNS hostname check succeeds but the local-DNS hostname check
    79	fails, configure the real application for proxy DNS/SOCKS5h. If the SOCKS curl
    80	checks work but the application fails, it is likely SOCKS-unaware, bypassing the
    81	proxy, using DoH, using a helper process, or preferring QUIC without TCP
    82	fallback.
    83	
    84	## Confirm the remote policy boundary
    85	
    86	The destination TCP socket is opened on the remote SSH machine by the
    87	authenticated `sshd` forwarding process/account. A successful command in a
    88	remote interactive shell therefore does not by itself prove that the `sshd`
    89	forwarding process is eligible for the same VPN or endpoint-security policy.
    90	
    91	For a working connection, all of the following must be true:
    92	
    93	- the remote VPN route includes the destination prefix and address family;
    94	- SSH forwarding policy permits the destination (`AllowTcpForwarding`,
    95	  `DisableForwarding`, `PermitOpen`, and key/certificate restrictions can deny
    96	  it);
    97	- a per-app, per-process, or per-user VPN/endpoint policy permits the
    98	  authenticated `sshd` forwarding process/account;
    99	- the remote firewall and destination service allow the selected remote source
   100	  address and TCP port.
   101	
   102	A remote route that points outside the VPN, an `administratively prohibited`
   103	SSH error, a timeout from only the SOCKS test, or a firewall denial each points
   104	to a remote policy or service owner. Correct that policy there; do not add
   105	source-Mac routes, PF rules, system proxy settings, or Network Extension code
   106	to Relux Proxy.
   107	
   108	## Protocol boundary
   109	
   110	OpenSSH dynamic forwarding accepts TCP SOCKS `CONNECT` requests. It does not
   111	carry UDP associations, ICMP/ping, multicast, broadcast, or arbitrary IP
   112	packets. QUIC/HTTP/3 is UDP-based and needs a TCP fallback to work through this
   113	tunnel. A successful `route -n get` on the source Mac is not expected to show a
   114	remote private route: the supported data plane is the per-application SOCKS
   115	connection, not a system tunnel.

